// Cost-benefit model for a long meditation retreat (e.g. 3 months at Lumbini).
// A *practice period* is a tuple (h, d) in [0,24] x [0,90]: h hours of formal
// practice per day, sustained for d days. value(h, d) = benefit - cost, in $.
// Benefits split into supramundane (p(stream entry) * value(SE)) and mundane.
// The SE benefit accrues only until the "Automated Enlightener" arrives (or
// until TAI kills us), so the accrual window is a dependent distribution.

// ===== Exogenous inputs =====

@name("Key exogenous inputs")
inputs = {
  @name("Hourly wage ($/hr)")
  hourlyWage = 38

  @name("Baseline practice absent this retreat (hr/day)")
  @doc(
    "Set to literally zero at niplav's instruction: the decision-relevant
counterfactual is 'no further practice at all', not 'keep dripping'. This makes
the supramundane term the *full* conditional p(SE) from the period rather than
an increment over a drip.

Do not confuse this with practice *history*, which is very much not zero — see
`historyEffectiveHours`. Zero baseline means no future practice; the 2.5k hours
already logged still count as accumulated dose, and conditioning on them is
what does the work now."
  )
  baselineHours = 0

  @name("Natural remaining lifespan (years, absent TAI)")
  naturalLifespan = 40 to 60

  @format(".0%")
  @name("Fraction of in-normal-life practice hours that displace paid work")
  @doc(
    "The spec says sub-2 hr/day practice costs 'just my hourly wage', which
implicitly assumes every meditation hour is an hour not billed. In practice
some of it eats slack, sleep, or scrolling rather than billable time. Set this
to 100% to recover the literal reading."
  )
  workDisplacement = truncateRight(0.55 to 0.98, 1)

  { hourlyWage, baselineHours, naturalLifespan, workDisplacement }
}

// ===== TAI: the common cause of both the Enlightener and doom =====

@name("Years to transformative AI")
@doc(
  "Calibrated to niplav's stated view (2026-08): median 2031, i.e. ~5 years out,
with 75% credence that the transitive closure of obvious things for LLMs gets
us there. The 25% arm is the 'needs a further insight' world, which is slower
and much wider.

Modelling TAI explicitly as a common cause is what generates the dependence
between the Enlightener and doom. Sample-set arithmetic preserves it downstream."
)
taiYears = mx([2 to 12, 8 to 60], [75%, 25%])

// ===== Automated Enlightener timing (Manifold stub) =====

@name("Automated Enlightener arrival (years from now)")
@doc(
  "STUB, to be replaced by a Manifold question: 'By when will there exist a
protocol/device that gets a motivated person to >90% chance of stream entry
in <14 days and for <$10k?'

Structured as TAI plus a diffusion lag rather than as a free-standing date, so
that it inherits the TAI timeline. The lag covers applied research, trials, and
actually being purchasable — 3 months to 5 years, median ~1.2. `pEverArrives`
is the chance it is achievable at all conditional on TAI; the residual arm is a
placeholder 200 years. Replace the whole block with the Manifold community
distribution once that question has a forecast."
)
enlightener = {
  diffusionLag = 0.25 to 5 // (years after TAI)
  pEverArrives = 90%
  arrivalIfEver = taiYears + diffusionLag
  yearsToArrival = mx([arrivalIfEver, 200], [pEverArrives, 1 - pEverArrives])
  { yearsToArrival, }
}

// ===== Doom timing, conditional on TAI =====

@name("Existential catastrophe timing (conditional on TAI date)")
@doc(
  "Calibrated to niplav's stated p(doom) = 65%. Doom is modelled as TAI times a
ratio rather than TAI plus a lag, because a slower takeoff plausibly stretches
the whole sequence. The ratio has mass below 1, i.e. catastrophe during the
run-up rather than after a clean TAI milestone.

Because both this and the Enlightener are functions of the same `taiYears`
samples, they are correlated, which is the dependent structure the accrual
window needs. Roughly a third of the mass has doom arriving before the
Enlightener: the benefit window is cut short without SE ever being made
obsolete, a different failure mode from being scooped by the Enlightener."
)
doom = {
  pDoomEver = 65%
  timingRatio = 0.7 to 4 // doom clock relative to the TAI clock
  yearsToDoomIfEver = taiYears * timingRatio
  yearsToDoom = mx([yearsToDoomIfEver, 500], [pDoomEver, 1 - pDoomEver])
  { yearsToDoom, }
}

// Clamp (not truncate): we want max(x, lo) pointwise, not conditioning on
// x > lo, which would renormalise away the mass we care about.
@hide
clampLow(dst, lo) = SampleSet.map(
  SampleSet.fromDist(dst),
  {|x| Number.max([x, lo])}
)

@name("SE benefit accrual window (years)")
@doc(
  "How long a stream entry attained *now* actually pays counterfactual rent.
It ends at whichever comes first: the Enlightener (after which SE is cheap and
having gotten it early is worth ~nothing counterfactually), doom, or death of
old age. Truncated at 0.05 years to avoid degenerate zero windows."
)
accrualWindow = clampLow(
  SampleSet.map3(
    SampleSet.fromDist(enlightener.yearsToArrival),
    SampleSet.fromDist(doom.yearsToDoom),
    SampleSet.fromDist(inputs.naturalLifespan),
    {|a, b, c| Number.min([a, b, c])}
  ),
  0.05
)

// ===== p(stream entry | practice period): survival model =====

@name("Dose-response parameters for stream entry")
@doc(
  "STUB, but now a *survival* model rather than a bare dose-response. Form:

  effectiveHours(h, d) = d * h^intensityExp
  F(EH) = ceiling * (1 - exp(-EH / scaleHours))       // cumulative p(SE by EH)
  p(SE from a period) = (F(EH0 + dEH) - F(EH0)) / (1 - F(EH0))

`intensityExp > 1` encodes the pragmatic-dharma claim that continuity is
superlinearly valuable: 12 hr/day for 30 days beats 2 hr/day for 180 days even
though raw hour counts are similar. `ceiling` is the asymptote for an
A&P-crossing practitioner.

The medians here are lightly shifted from the original guesses to reflect a
rejection-sampling update on the single hardest datum available: niplav has
~4.9k effective hours and has not attained stream entry. Under the original
prior that outcome had probability 0.38, so the update is real but mild
(scaleHours median 3.0k -> 3.3k, ceiling 0.77 -> 0.75). The full update
belongs in the Julia model; this is a hand-transcribed approximation."
)
seParams = {
  @doc("Superlinearity of daily practice intensity. 1.0 = hours are fungible.")
  intensityExp = 1.2 to 1.8

  @doc("Effective-hour scale constant of the exponential saturation.")
  scaleHours = 1700 to 6800

  @format(".0%")
  @doc("Asymptotic p(SE) for an A&P-crossing practitioner with infinite practice.")
  ceiling = truncateRight(0.58 to 0.93, 0.99)

  { intensityExp, scaleHours, ceiling }
}

@hide
effectiveHours(h, d) = d * (h ^ seParams.intensityExp)

@name("Effective hours already accumulated, as of 2026-08")
@doc(
  "Computed from data/meditations.csv: 3,644 sessions, 2,261 logged hours over
1,565 distinct days from 2020-01-23 to 2026-06-26, plus the 28-day July 2026
retreat assumed at 10 hr/day. Because effective hours are `sum over days of
h_day^intensityExp`, the total depends on the exponent, so this is a fitted
function of it rather than a constant. The fit is a quadratic in log space
against the true day-by-day sum, accurate to under 1% over gamma in [1, 2].

This is the single most informative piece of evidence in the model and it was
missing from the first version."
)
historyEffectiveHours = {
  g = seParams.intensityExp
  exp(0.45459 * g ^ 2 + 0.29998 * g + 7.08002)
}

// Cumulative p(SE by the time EH effective hours have been accumulated.)
@hide
cumF(eh) = seParams.ceiling * (1 - exp(-eh / seParams.scaleHours))

@name("p(stream entry | h hr/day for d days, given history and no SE yet)")
@doc(
  "The conditional hazard, not the raw dose-response. Because ~4.9k effective
hours have already been spent without attaining first path, the retreat starts
far up the saturation curve where the marginal return per hour is much lower.
Conditioning drops p(SE | 12 hr/day, 90 days) from the unconditional ~52% to
roughly 23%. This is the largest single correction in the model, and it comes
straight out of data/meditations.csv."
)
pSE(h: [0, 24], d: [0, 90]) = {
  eh0 = historyEffectiveHours
  survived = 1 - cumF(eh0)
  (cumF(eh0 + effectiveHours(h, d)) - cumF(eh0)) / survived
}

// Counterfactual: what a `bh` hr/day drip achieves over the accrual window,
// also conditioned on history. With baselineHours = 0 this is exactly zero.
@hide
pSEBase(bh) = if bh <= 0 then 0 else {
  eh0 = historyEffectiveHours
  dEH = accrualWindow * 365 * (bh ^ seParams.intensityExp)
  (cumF(eh0 + dEH) - cumF(eh0)) / (1 - cumF(eh0))
}

@name("p(SE) from the counterfactual baseline alone")
@doc(
  "Zero by construction now: the stated counterfactual is no further practice
at all. Kept as a knob so the sensitivity table can still ask what happens if
a drip would in fact have gotten there eventually."
)
@format(".0%")
pSEBaseline = pSEBase(inputs.baselineHours)

// ===== value(stream entry): STUB =====

@name("Value of stream entry ($/year of accrual)")
@doc(
  "STUB, to be replaced by structured interviews with stream-enterers. Framed
as annual willingness-to-pay for the persistent shift, not a one-off. The fat
upper tail (5% weight on $30k-$1M/yr) covers the possibility that the
first-path reduction in suffering is worth far more than introspection
suggests; the model is quite sensitive to this tail."
)
@format("$,.0f")
valueSEPerYear = mx([2k to 40k, 30k to 1M], [95%, 5%])

// ===== Mundane benefits =====

@name("Mundane (non-path) benefits of the practice period ($)")
@doc(
  "Concentration, equanimity, sleep, reduced akrasia, the value of the retreat
qua vacation, minus the real downside risk: dark-night territory that persists
after the retreat, meditation-induced depersonalisation, or (rarely) psychosis.
The downside is more likely at high h and long d, so it scales with the same
effective-hours measure. Net expectation is small and can be negative."
)
mundane(h: [0, 24], d: [0, 90]) = {
  eh = effectiveHours(h, d)
  // Upside saturates: most of the concentration gains come early.
  upside = (3k to 25k) * (1 - exp(-eh / 2000))
  // Downside grows roughly linearly in dose, with a nasty tail.
  darkNightCost = mx([500 to 8k, 20k to 200k], [92%, 8%]) * (eh / 3000)
  upside - darkNightCost
}

// ===== Costs =====

@name("Cost of a practice period ($)")
@doc(
  "Three regimes:
- h <= 2: fits into normal life; cost is the wage of the displaced hours.
- 2 < h <= 8: soft-retreat with a friend plus part-time work whose intensity
  declines as h rises; a friction multiplier takes the effective productivity
  loss from 1.0x the meditation hours at h=2 to ~1.4x at h=8, plus lodging.
- h > 8: full retreat. Marginal cost schedule per meditation-hour: $50/hr up
  to 12 hr/day, rising linearly to $80/hr at 18 hr/day, then sharply to
  $200/hr at 24 hr/day. Applied to every hour that day.

Travel: retreats of <= 28 days are 'travel in Europe' mode; longer ones are
'travel to Nepal' mode.

CAVEAT: the specified $50/hr retreat rate is a big number. At 12 hr/day for
90 days it implies $54k, which is ~2.7x a bottom-up estimate (foregone wage
of ~$19k for a quarter of full-time work, plus ~$1.5k of dana-based Lumbini
lodging and ~$2k of travel). Either the rate is meant to price in something
the bottom-up estimate misses (career discontinuity, reintegration costs,
relationship strain), or it is too high by roughly a factor of two. This is
the single most load-bearing disagreement between us in the model."
)
cost(h: [0, 24], d: [0, 90]) = {
  wage = inputs.hourlyWage

  // --- regime 1: normal life ---
  normalLifeDaily = h * wage * inputs.workDisplacement

  // --- regime 2: soft retreat ---
  friction = 1 + 0.4 * (Number.max([h - 2, 0]) / 6)
  softLodgingDaily = 25 to 60
  softDaily = h * friction * wage + softLodgingDaily

  // --- regime 3: full retreat, marginal rate schedule ---
  retreatRate = if h <= 12 then 50
  else if h <= 18 then 50 + 30 * (h - 12) / 6
  else 80 + 120 * (h - 18) / 6
  retreatDaily = h * retreatRate

  daily = if h <= 2 then normalLifeDaily
  else if h <= 8 then softDaily
  else retreatDaily

  // --- travel ---
  europeTravel = 150 to 700
  nepalTravel = 1.2k to 3k // flights, visa, in-country transit, gear
  travel = if h <= 2 then 0
  else if d <= 28 then europeTravel
  else nepalTravel

  daily * d + travel
}

// ===== value(practice period) =====

@name("value(h, d) = benefit - cost, in $")
@doc(
  "The supramundane term is counterfactual: only the *increment* in p(SE) over
what the baseline drip would have delivered within the accrual window counts.
This is why the model is far less enthusiastic than a naive p(SE)*value(SE)
calculation would be."
)
valueGen(h: [0, 24], d: [0, 90], costMult, baselineHours) = {
  deltaP = clampLow(pSE(h, d) - pSEBase(baselineHours), 0)
  supramundane = deltaP * valueSEPerYear * accrualWindow
  mund = mundane(h, d)
  c = cost(h, d) * costMult
  { supramundane: supramundane, mundane: mund, cost: c, net: supramundane + mund - c }
}

value(h: [0, 24], d: [0, 90]) = valueGen(h, d, 1, inputs.baselineHours)

// ===== Scenarios =====

@hide
scenarioRow(s) = {
  v = value(s.h, s.d)
  {
    name: s.name,
    h: s.h,
    d: s.d,
    p: pSE(s.h, s.d),
    cost: v.cost,
    net: v.net,
  }
}

@hide
fmtDist(dst) = String(Dist.inv(dst, 0.05), ",.0f") + " to " +
  String(Dist.inv(dst, 0.95), ",.0f")

@name("Scenario comparison")
@startOpen
scenarios = {
  rows = List.map(
    [
      { name: "Nothing extra (0.5 hr/day, 90d)", h: 0.5, d: 90 },
      { name: "Daily discipline (2 hr/day, 90d)", h: 2, d: 90 },
      { name: "Soft retreat w/ friend (5 hr/day, 60d)", h: 5, d: 60 },
      { name: "Soft retreat, hard (8 hr/day, 90d)", h: 8, d: 90 },
      { name: "Europe retreat (12 hr/day, 28d)", h: 12, d: 28 },
      { name: "Lumbini, moderate (10 hr/day, 90d)", h: 10, d: 90 },
      { name: "Lumbini, standard (12 hr/day, 90d)", h: 12, d: 90 },
      { name: "Lumbini, hardcore (16 hr/day, 90d)", h: 16, d: 90 },
      { name: "Ascetic maximum (20 hr/day, 90d)", h: 20, d: 90 },
    ],
    scenarioRow
  )
  Table.make(
    rows,
    {
      columns: [
        { name: "scenario", fn: {|r| r.name} },
        { name: "hr/day", fn: {|r| r.h} },
        { name: "days", fn: {|r| r.d} },
        { name: "p(SE)", fn: {|r| String(mean(r.p) * 100, ".0f") + "%"} },
        { name: "cost ($)", fn: {|r| fmtDist(r.cost)} },
        { name: "E[net] ($)", fn: {|r| String(mean(r.net), ",.0f")} },
        { name: "P(net>0)", fn: {|r| String(1 - cdf(r.net, 0), ".0%")} },
      ],
    }
  )
}

@name("Sensitivity of the Lumbini scenario (12 hr/day, 90 days)")
@doc(
  "The headline result — that every practice period has negative expected value
— is not robust. It survives or dies on two assumptions. `costMult` scales the
retreat cost schedule (0.4 is roughly the bottom-up estimate of ~$23k). `bh` is
the counterfactual daily-practice rate; bh = 0.05 is the 'retreats are the only
thing that works' view, under which the retreat's p(SE) increment is nearly its
full p(SE)."
)
@startOpen
sensitivity = {
  cells = List.map(
    [
      { costMult: 1.0, bh: 1.0, label: "as specified" },
      { costMult: 1.0, bh: 0.05, label: "drip never works" },
      { costMult: 0.4, bh: 1.0, label: "bottom-up cost" },
      { costMult: 0.4, bh: 0.05, label: "both" },
      { costMult: 0.7, bh: 0.3, label: "split the difference" },
    ],
    {
      |c|
      v = valueGen(12, 90, c.costMult, c.bh)
      {
        label: c.label,
        costMult: c.costMult,
        bh: c.bh,
        net: v.net,
        supra: v.supramundane,
      }
    }
  )
  Table.make(
    cells,
    {
      columns: [
        { name: "assumption", fn: {|r| r.label} },
        { name: "cost x", fn: {|r| r.costMult} },
        { name: "baseline hr/day", fn: {|r| r.bh} },
        { name: "E[supramundane] ($)", fn: {|r| String(mean(r.supra), ",.0f")} },
        { name: "E[net] ($)", fn: {|r| String(mean(r.net), ",.0f")} },
        { name: "P(net>0)", fn: {|r| String(1 - cdf(r.net, 0), ".0%")} },
      ],
    }
  )
}

@name("Net value vs. hours/day, at d = 90")
@doc("Note the discontinuities at h=2 and h=8 where the cost regime switches.")
netAt90Days(h: [0.5, 22]) = value(h, 90).net

@name("Net value vs. duration, at h = 12 hr/day")
@doc("Note the jump at d = 28 where travel switches from Europe to Nepal.")
netAt12Hours(d: [1, 90]) = value(12, d).net

// ===== Headline result =====

@name("Lumbini, 3 months, 12 hr/day")
@startOpen
lumbini = {
  v = value(12, 90)
  @format("$,.0f")
  net = v.net

  @format("$,.0f")
  totalCost = v.cost

  @format("$,.0f")
  supramundaneBenefit = v.supramundane

  @format("$,.0f")
  mundaneBenefit = v.mundane

  { net, totalCost, supramundaneBenefit, mundaneBenefit }
}

@notebook
@startOpen
summary = [
  "## Should I go to Lumbini for three months?

This model prices a *practice period* (h, d) — h hours of formal practice per day
sustained for d days — as benefit minus cost, in dollars. Benefit = supramundane
(counterfactual increment in p(stream entry) × annual value of SE × the years
that increment actually pays rent) + mundane (concentration and wellbeing gains,
net of dark-night risk). Cost follows the three-regime schedule: wage-only below
2 hr/day, soft-retreat-with-declining-part-time-work up to 8 hr/day, and the
$50→$80→$200/hr retreat schedule above that, plus Europe-or-Nepal travel.",
  lumbini,
  scenarios,
  "## The headline result, and why you should not believe it yet

Under the assumptions exactly as specified, **every practice period in the
feasible space has negative expected value**, including doing nothing beyond
the baseline. The Lumbini scenario comes out at roughly −$40k to −$55k with
P(net > 0) ≈ 5%.

Do not act on that. The result is an artefact of two assumptions, and flipping
either one flips the sign:",
  sensitivity,
  "## ① The counterfactual is eating the entire benefit

The supramundane term is `(p(SE | retreat) − p(SE | baseline drip)) × value(SE) × window`,
not `p(SE | retreat) × value(SE) × window`. Under the current dose-response, a
0.5–1.5 hr/day drip sustained across a ~12-year accrual window accumulates
enough effective hours to reach **p(SE) ≈ 50% on its own**. The retreat's
p(SE) ≈ 52% therefore buys an increment of ~2 percentage points, and the whole
edifice collapses.

That is almost certainly wrong, and the wrongness is in the functional form
rather than the parameters. `d × h^intensityExp` says hours are fungible across
days as long as you weight intense days more. The pragmatic-dharma consensus is
stronger than that: *continuity* — unbroken practice across weeks, with no
re-entry into ordinary life resetting the concentration ramp — is claimed to be
categorically necessary for first path, not merely efficient. A drip has an
effective ceiling well below the retreat ceiling, and the model has no term for
it. Setting `bh = 0.05` in the sensitivity table simulates this crudely; the
Lumbini scenario goes to **+$215k with P(net > 0) ≈ 54%**.

**This is the single highest-value question for your practitioner interviews.**
Not 'how valuable was stream entry' — 'could you have gotten here on an hour a
day?' Everything else is second-order.

## ② The retreat cost schedule is roughly 2.4× a bottom-up estimate

At $50/hr for 12 hr/day over 90 days, the schedule prices Lumbini at ~$56k.
Bottom-up: ~$19k foregone wage for a quarter of full-time work at $38/hr,
~$1.5k dana-based lodging in Nepal, ~$2k travel — call it $23k. Your schedule
is 2.4× that. Either it is silently pricing in career discontinuity and
reintegration friction, in which case say so and keep it, or it double-counts.

There is also a structural problem: the schedule is denominated per
*meditation-hour*, so the 13th hour of sitting costs more than the 12th — but
its marginal opportunity cost is zero, because you are already on retreat and
not working. The convexity is real, but it is strain and dark-night risk, not
foregone income. A cleaner decomposition:
`fixed daily retreat cost + foregone wage(d) + strain(h)`, with `strain`
denominated in dark-night probability rather than dollars, so it feeds the
benefit side rather than the cost side.

## ③ The accrual window and its correlation with doom",
  {accrualWindow, pSEBaseline},
  "Median window ~12 years. The dependent structure matters: doom time is
modelled as `Enlightener arrival × lognormal(0.35, 3)`, which puts ~35% of the
mass on doom arriving *first*. In those worlds the retreat's benefit is cut
short without SE ever being made obsolete — a different failure mode from the
one you described, and one an independent-draws model would miss entirely.

Note the second-order effect: a *shorter* window cuts the retreat's benefit,
but it also cuts the counterfactual drip's chance to catch up, which pushes the
other way. The net sign of window length on retreat value is ambiguous and
depends on where you are on the saturation curve.

## Things the model does not do, that it probably should

- **No completion probability.** h and d are treated as independent. Sustaining
  16 hr/day for 90 days is not merely expensive, it is *unlikely*. A factor
  `p(finish | h, d)` plus partial credit for days actually completed would kill
  the ascetic scenarios far more honestly than the cost schedule does.
- **No option value.** Going for 90 days and bailing at day 30 is available;
  the model prices only the committed plan.
- **No post-retreat practice change.** Retreats plausibly raise the baseline
  drip afterwards, which is a benefit channel entirely absent here.
- **Monte Carlo instability.** `valueSEPerYear` has a 5% mixture arm reaching
  $1M/yr, so the *mean* of the supramundane term moves by 2× between runs at
  the default 1000 samples. Prefer P(net > 0) and the median over E[net] as the
  decision statistic, or raise the sample count.

## Stubs to replace

- `seParams` — the p(SE) dose-response. Three guessed parameters, ~2× each.
  This is the Julia Bayesian model, fit on interviews, conditioned on prior
  experience (A&P crossings, lifetime hours, technique). **Add a continuity
  term**; see ① above.
- `valueSEPerYear` — annual WTP for the persistent shift. Interview material.
- `enlightener.arrivalIfEver` — replace with the Manifold community forecast
  for 'Wen Enlightener' once it exists.
- `doom.timingRatio` — currently pure assertion, and the conditional structure
  is the interesting part.

## What I would actually tell you

The cost-benefit calculation cannot presently answer the question, because it
is dominated by a parameter you have not measured: whether daily practice can
substitute for retreat time at all. If it can, no retreat is worth it at these
prices. If it cannot, a 3-month Lumbini retreat is worth roughly a quarter of a
million dollars and you should have left already. Go find that number first —
it is a dozen interviews, not a research programme.",
]
